<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biswa Singha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1515–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biswa Singha was the founder of the Koch kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in the early 16th century. Uniting Koch chiefs after the fall of the Kamata kingdom, he made his capital at Kamatapur and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, inviting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> to his court. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a> raised the kingdom to its height, and his line ruled Cooch Behar until 1949.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>After the Kamarupa kingdom broke up in the 12th century, the Kamata kingdom rose in the west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, with the lands of the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyan</a> landlords forming a buffer to the east. In 1498 Alauddin Hussain Shah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, conquered Kamata, but his son Danyal Husayn was driven out by a confederation of Bhuyans who then kept their own fiefdoms and founded no kingdom.</p><p>Bisu, as he was first known, was born late in the 15th century to Hariya Mandal of Mount Chikna, in present-day Kokrajhar district, head of twelve Mech families, and to Hira, daughter of the Koch leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajo">Hajo</a>. Taking up his grandfather's political legacy, he became chief of the eastern Koches in the Khuntaghat region, probably around 1509, and fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> occupation. From about 1509 he united the Mech, Koch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutia">Bhutias</a> of several districts and defeated the Bhuyan chiefs one by one, the last of them at Pandu near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Brahman priests gave his rule legitimacy with a legend that Bisu was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and granted him kshatriya status. He took the name Biswa Singha, and the royal family the name Rajbanshi; this, in 1515, is taken as the start of Koch rule in Kamata. He made his brother Sisu yuvaraj, created twelve ministers called Karjis from the leading Mech families, and after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> organised the able-bodied men as Paiks under graded commanders.</p><p>In 1532-1533 the Ahom general Tonkham Borgohain, pursuing the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> commander Turbak to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatoya-river">Karatoya</a> river, restored Biswa Singha at Kamata as a buffer between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> and Bengal. Resenting this <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, he attempted an invasion of the Ahom kingdom in about 1537, abandoned it for lack of supplies, and agreed instead to pay an annual tribute. He sent his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a> and Sukladhwaj, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a>, to study under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholar Brahmananda at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, and brought priests from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> and Puri to his court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Darrangraj Vamshavali says he reigned for 25 years and died of sores; the Kochbiharer Itihas says he died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. On his deathbed he urged his successors to throw off the Ahom vassalage. By breaking the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> he left a centralised monarchy for his son to expand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biswa Singha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Koch kingdom rose under <b>Biswa Singha</b> in the early 16th century and reached its height under Nara Narayan.</blockquote><a href="/state/cooch-behar-state">Cooch Behar State</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Bengal Sultanate destroyed the Kamata Kingdom around 1498, <b>Biswa Singha</b>, a leader of the Koch people, united the local chiefs and founded a new state around 1515, centred on the Cooch Behar area.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-kingdom">Koch Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
